{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:inflation:outside","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:a09490fa0e3a2e92431d6efc9415c8560caa6e1d27d84df11d18c5aeb1e03005","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.55},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.8},{"probability":0.9,"value":3.15}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Reference class 1: G7 GDP-weighted 10y real yield by decade. 1970s ~-1 to -2% (nominal ~8% vs inflation ~9%); 1980s ~+4.5-5%; 1990s ~+4% (US TIPS debut 1997 at 3.4-3.8%); 2000s ~+2% (US TIPS avg ~2.0-2.2%); 2010s ~+0.4% (US ~0.5%, JP ~0, DE negative); 2020-2025 ~+0.3% (deeply negative 2020-21, +1.5-2.2% 2023-25). GDP weighting matters: US ~50% of G7, Japan ~10%, Germany ~10%, with JP ~-0.3 to +0.5% and DE ~0.3-0.6% real, so the G7 aggregate sits well below the US print. With US 10y TIPS at ~2.60% (locked, Oct 30 2026), the G7 GDP-weighted level today is ~1.7-1.9%.\nReference class 2 (base rate of staying near the starting level): consecutive decade changes were +6.0, -1.0, -2.0, -1.6, -0.1pp — mean absolute change ~2.1pp, and only 2 of 5 transitions stayed within +/-100bp. So \"decade-average real yield near its starting level\" has a base rate of only ~40%, yet it remains the modal single cell.\nReference class 3 (high-debt resolution, the class the locked debt arithmetic selects): US/UK post-1945 with debt >100% of GDP resolved through financial repression, with G7 real yields ~0 to negative for ~25 years. Against it, the 1980s-90s high-term-premium class gives +3-5%. These two classes bracket the distribution and are why it is wide and right-skewed rather than tight.\nArithmetic adjustments from 2A, all accounting rather than mechanism: gross public debt >100% of GDP by 2029 (~142% US, ~127% China by 2031), net interest 2%->3% of GDP